{% comment %}
  Prints a heading or a line of display copy, optionally honouring the line
  breaks the merchant asked for.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. Every heading across the PDP sections is a `text` setting,
  which is a single-line box in the theme editor - there is no key you can press
  in one to produce a line break. So "Made in small batches." could never be set
  as two lines however the merchant typed it, and a heading that wraps badly on
  a phone had nowhere to be fixed except by shortening the words.

  Two ways in, because one of them has to work in a box that cannot hold a
  newline:

  - a real line break, for the settings that are `textarea`
  - a typed `<br>`, for the settings that are `text` - `<br>`, `<br/>` and
    `<br />` all count, in any case

  Both are behind the section's own *Allow line breaks in headings* checkbox,
  which is off by default. That is the point of the switch: with it off a
  merchant who types `<br>` sees `<br>` on the page and knows immediately that
  it was not meant to be markup, rather than having the theme silently accept
  HTML in a field that is documented as plain text.

  SAFETY. The text is escaped first and the breaks are put back afterwards, so
  the only markup that can ever reach the page from one of these fields is the
  `<br>` this snippet writes itself. Anything else a merchant types - a
  `<script>`, an `<img onerror>`, a stray angle bracket - is still printed as
  characters. That ordering is the whole guarantee; do not be tempted to
  shorten it by skipping the escape when the checkbox is on.

  Accepts:
  - text:         {String} the setting's value.
  - allow_breaks: {Boolean} the section's checkbox.

  Usage:
  {% render 'pdp-heading', text: section.settings.heading, allow_breaks: allow_breaks %}
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign out = text | escape

  if allow_breaks
    assign out = out | newline_to_br
    assign out = out | replace: '&lt;br&gt;', '<br>'
    assign out = out | replace: '&lt;br/&gt;', '<br>'
    assign out = out | replace: '&lt;br /&gt;', '<br>'
    assign out = out | replace: '&lt;BR&gt;', '<br>'
    assign out = out | replace: '&lt;BR/&gt;', '<br>'
    assign out = out | replace: '&lt;BR /&gt;', '<br>'
  endif
-%}
{{- out -}}
